<SiteLayout title="JPG vs PNG: Which Image Format Should You Use? | ConvertAnyFile" description="A practical JPG vs PNG guide covering photos, screenshots, transparency, file size, and what conversion can and cannot improve.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>JPG vs PNG</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Image formats</span><h1>JPG vs PNG: two formats for different jobs.</h1><p>JPG is usually the practical choice for photographs and small delivery files. PNG is usually the practical choice for lossless graphics, screenshots, and transparency.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-shell"><article class="site-container article"><h2>Start with the content, not the extension</h2><p>There is no universal winner between JPG and PNG. The right choice depends on what the image contains and what you need to do with it next. A photograph with millions of subtle color transitions benefits from the compact lossy compression of JPEG. A logo, interface screenshot, diagram, or transparent graphic often benefits from PNG's lossless storage and alpha channel.</p><h2>Where JPG is strong</h2><p>JPEG was designed around photographic imagery. It can remove visual information that people are less likely to notice and produce a much smaller file than an uncompressed image. That makes JPG useful for websites, email attachments, camera exports, social uploads, and general sharing.</p><p>The tradeoff is that JPEG is lossy. Re-saving an already compressed JPG at a new quality setting can introduce another generation of compression artifacts. JPG also has no alpha transparency channel.</p><h2>Where PNG is strong</h2><p>PNG uses lossless compression. Re-encoding a PNG does not deliberately throw away pixel information in the way a normal JPEG encode does. It also supports transparency, which makes it useful for interface assets, logos, overlays, screenshots, and graphics with sharp edges.</p><p>For a full-resolution photograph, PNG can be dramatically larger than JPG because it is trying to preserve the pixel values rather than approximate them with a lossy photo codec.</p><h2>What happens when you convert JPG to PNG?</h2><p>The new PNG can store the current pixels without another lossy JPEG generation, but it cannot restore detail that JPEG compression already removed. The output may be larger while looking almost identical to the source JPG.</p><h2>What happens when you convert PNG to JPG?</h2><p>The output becomes lossy and loses any true alpha transparency. For photographs this can be a useful tradeoff because JPG may be much smaller. For logos, text, and UI screenshots, compression artifacts around hard edges can make JPG a worse fit.</p><div class="callout"><strong>A practical rule</strong><p>Use JPG for photographic delivery. Use PNG when lossless graphics or transparency matter. Keep an original master if the image is important, and create delivery copies for each destination.</p></div><p><a class="related-card" href="/png-to-jpg/">Convert PNG to JPG →</a> <a class="related-card" href="/jpg-to-png/">Convert JPG to PNG →</a></p></article></section>
